values (
  6,
  'English',
  $bow$# Grade 6 English — Budget of Work

Grade Level Standard: learners demonstrate applied and critical literacy in consolidating receptive and productive skills; use literal and implied meanings in composing literary and informational texts; use a range of reference materials to compose narrative, expository, and persuasive texts with simple, compound, and complex sentences; create simple survey forms for specific purposes; use tone/mood and verbal/non-verbal cues appropriate to age, gender, and culture; and use visual and multimedia elements to derive meaning from and produce multimedia texts for specific purposes. All four quarters share the same purpose/context focus: culture-based texts (Indigenous Peoples and regional celebrations).

## Term 1 (Q1: Narrative and Persuasive Texts)
- **Listening and Reading (Literary):** Note important story-grammar elements; sequence at least 8 events; identify flashback plot; analyze hyperbole and irony to get and clarify meaning; infer the author's/speaker's purpose, message, and target audience; make predictions about a story's possible ending; draw conclusions; identify the main idea; summarize story events; apply story elements to one's own schema by learning vicariously from the text.
- **Listening and Reading (Informational):** Note important information using inductive-deductive (diamond) outlining; identify persuasive text type; identify the author's purpose (entertain, inform/explain/describe, persuade); draw conclusions, make generalizations, summarize; distinguish fact from opinion; identify propaganda techniques — name calling/labelling, glittering generalities, transfer.
- **Speaking and Writing:** Use context clues from punctuation; use an almanac as a general reference; use intransitive verbs; use present/past/future perfect tenses; use prepositional phrases as adjectives; use order of adverbs (manner, place, frequency, time, purpose); use noun/pronoun/adjective complements; compose compound-complex sentences; provide evidence to support information in a text with introduction, body, and conclusion; express ideas using narrative and persuasive text types; use facial expressions, gestures, eye contact, haptics, posture, and proxemics/blocking appropriately; create a simple printed personal-data survey form.
- **Viewing and Representing:** Identify the purpose of a visual text and analyze how visual elements contribute to its meaning; evaluate cultural appropriateness of visual elements; create an original visual text; identify multimedia elements (video, two- and three-dimensional animation); derive meaning from multimedia by identifying the author's purpose, analyzing how multimedia elements contribute to meaning, and determining the author's point of view/stand and propaganda technique used; create a multimedia text.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner locate one real regional or Indigenous Peoples' celebration observed in their own community, describe its purpose and traditions in a short persuasive paragraph encouraging others to attend, and identify which propaganda technique (if any) they deliberately avoided using to keep the paragraph honest.

## Term 2 (Q2: Narrative and Persuasive Texts, continued)
- **Listening and Reading (Literary):** Same subdomain progression as Q1 — story grammar, sequencing at least 8 events, flashback plot, irony, inferring author's purpose/message/target audience, predicting possible endings, drawing conclusions, main idea, summary, learning vicariously.
- **Listening and Reading (Informational):** Diamond outlining, persuasive text type, author's purpose, drawing conclusions/generalizations/summary, fact vs. opinion, propaganda techniques — testimonials/testimonials, plain folks, bandwagon.
- **Speaking and Writing:** Use directories (acronyms/abbreviations used by organizations) as a general reference; intransitive verbs; perfect tenses; prepositional phrases as adjectives; noun/pronoun/adjective complements; compound-complex sentences; provide evidence in narrative/persuasive writing; non-verbal cues; create an open-ended printed survey form.
- **Viewing and Representing:** Same visual/multimedia progression as Q1 (purpose, cultural appropriateness, creation, video/animation, author's purpose and point of view/propaganda in multimedia, multimedia creation).

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner design and administer a real open-ended survey (at least 5 people) asking what a regional celebration means to them, then summarize the actual answers received — not invented ones — in a short written report.

## Term 3 (Q3: Narrative and Persuasive Texts, continued)
- **Listening and Reading (Literary):** Same progression — story grammar, sequencing at least 8 events, flashback plot, irony, inferring purpose/message/target audience, predicting endings, drawing conclusions, main idea, summary, vicarious learning.
- **Listening and Reading (Informational):** Diamond outlining, persuasive text type, author's purpose, conclusions/generalizations/summary, fact vs. opinion, propaganda techniques — fear, half-truths/spin.
- **Speaking and Writing:** Use handbooks and manuals as a general reference; intransitive verbs; perfect tenses; prepositional phrases as adverbs; noun/pronoun/adjective complements; compound-complex sentences; provide evidence in writing; non-verbal cues; create an oral interview survey form.
- **Viewing and Representing:** Same visual/multimedia progression as previous quarters.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner conduct a real oral interview (using their own interview survey form) with a family or community member about a personal experience tied to a regional celebration, then write up the interview's actual findings.

## Term 4 (Q4: Narrative and Persuasive Texts, continued)
- **Listening and Reading (Literary):** Same progression — story grammar, sequencing at least 8 events, flashback plot, irony, inferring purpose/message/target audience, predicting endings, drawing conclusions, main idea, summary, vicarious learning.
- **Listening and Reading (Informational):** Diamond outlining, persuasive text type, author's purpose, conclusions/generalizations/summary, fact vs. opinion, propaganda techniques — bad logic/unwarranted extrapolation, card stacking.
- **Speaking and Writing:** Intransitive verbs; perfect tenses; prepositional phrases as adverbs; noun/pronoun/adjective complements; compound-complex sentences; express ideas using narrative and persuasive text types; non-verbal cues; create a digital/online survey form.
- **Viewing and Representing:** Same visual/multimedia progression as previous quarters, culminating the year's multimedia text creation.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner build one real online survey form (using an actual free tool such as Google Forms with adult help) about a regional/Indigenous Peoples' celebration, distribute it to real respondents, and report the genuine response data collected — not projected or invented numbers.$bow$,
